When Mendeleev first invented his periodic table, he was not expecting chemical elements like Osmium or Iridium because they were still being discovered until much later. Although he did believe that they should be placed in appropriate positions based on their properties, he could not determine exact atomic weights at the time. He proposed that these elements would fall into specific places based on their potential.


As time passed and more elements were discovered, this became possible, but not without some limitations. For example, many metals were found to contain compounds which were not known at the time, creating confusion and limitations in classifying elements precisely. Eventually, scientists learned that atoms existed and that elements can vary in terms of their size, volume, and density.

In recent decades, with the development of advanced technologies like spectrometry and mass spectrometry, it has become feasible to accurately determine the atomic weight and other physical properties of individual elements. This allowed greater precision in classification systems. Today, modern chemistry studies show how even small differences in the composition of atoms can significantly affect their behavior and reactivity.

The periodic table now contains over 118 elements, including all the elemental substances currently known to science, and continues to evolve as new discoveries come about. Despite the advances made in understanding atomic structures and the behavior of chemical elements, there are still mysteries surrounding the nature of matter itself. Scientists remain curious about the fundamental forces that govern the interactions between particles at the atomic level.

Through research and experimentation, ongoing investigations continue to uncover new knowledge and deepen our appreciation for the complexity of the natural world. As technology improves, we may yet find answers to the many unanswered questions about matter's structure, its interactions, and its ultimate fate.

